Question.en:
**(a)** Let $M_n(F)$ be the set of all $n\times n$ matrices over a field $F$. Find all possible Rational Canonical Forms for:

**(i)** matrices in $M_6(\mathbb{R})$ with minimal polynomial $(x^2+1)^2(x-1)$;
**(ii)** matrices in $M_{15}(\mathbb{Q})$ with minimal polynomial $(x^2+x+1)^2(x^3+2)^2$;
**(iii)** matrices in $M_8(\mathbb{F}_2)$ with minimal polynomial $x^5+1$.

**(b)** True or false:
**(i)** there exists $A\in M_5(\mathbb{F}_3)$ with minimal polynomial $x^4+1$;
**(ii)** there exists $A\in M_5(\mathbb{F}_3)$ with minimal polynomial $x^4+x^2+1$.

Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional inner product space over $F=\mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{C}$. Prove the following statements:

**(i)** if $v,w\in V$ are such that $(u,v)=(u,w)$ for all $u\in V$, then $v=w$;
**(ii)** Pythagoras: if $(u,v)=0$, then $\|u+v\|^2=\|u\|^2+\|v\|^2$;
**(iii)** triangle inequalities: if $u,v,w\in V$ then $\|u+v\|\le\|u\|+\|v\|$, and $\|u-v\|\le\|u-w\|+\|w-v\|$;
**(iv)** any orthogonal set $v_1,\ldots,v_r$ of nonzero vectors is linearly independent;
**(v)** if $\|u\|=\|v\|=(u,v)=1$, then $u=v$;
**(vi)** if $W$ is a subspace of $V$, then $(W^\perp)^\perp=W$.

Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional inner product space, and let $S,T:V\to V$ be linear maps, with adjoints $S^*,T^*$. Prove:

**(i)** $(S+T)^*=S^*+T^*$;
**(ii)** $(\lambda T)^*=\overline\lambda\,T^*$ for any scalar $\lambda$;
**(iii)** $(T^*)^*=T$;
**(iv)** $(ST)^*=T^*S^*$;
**(v)** $\ker(T^*)=\mathrm{Im}(T)^\perp$;
**(vi)** $\mathrm{Im}(T^*)=\ker(T)^\perp$.

Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional inner product space over $F$, where $F=\mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{C}$. We call a linear map $S:V\to V$ an isometry if $(S(u),S(v))=(u,v)$ for all $u,v\in V$. For an isometry $S$, prove:

**(i)** $\|S(v)\|=\|v\|$ for all $v\in V$;
**(ii)** $S^*S=SS^*=I_V$;
**(iii)** the adjoint $S^*$ is also an isometry;
**(iv)** if $\mathcal{B}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V$, then the matrix $[S]_{\mathcal{B}}$ is unitary;
**(v)** show that if $F=\mathbb{C}$, then there is an orthonormal basis $E$ of $V$ such that the matrix $[S]_E$ is diagonal. (Hint: try to copy the proof of the spectral theorem 15.3.)

Let $V$ be the vector space over $\mathbb{R}$ consisting of polynomials in $x$ of degree at most 3. Which of the following are bilinear forms on $V$? Which of these are symmetric, skew-symmetric or non-degenerate?

**(i)** $(f,g)=\int_0^1f(x)g(x)\,dx$ for all $f,g\in V$;
**(ii)** $(f,g)=f(1)+g(1)$ for all $f,g\in V$;
**(iii)** $(f,g)=f(1)g(1)$ for all $f,g\in V$;
**(iv)** $(f,g)=f(1)g'(1)-f'(1)g(1)+f(0)g'(0)-f'(0)g(0)$ for all $f,g\in V$.
